- op: create  # 8620
  at: '2026-05-17T19:18:45Z'  # 8620
  by: agent  # 8620
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 8620
  clock: 1  # 8620
  nonce: '8620'  # 8620
  data:  # 8620
    kind: invariant  # 8620
    title: "META: Symbol-name lookup must disambiguate — kind-segregated registry OR calibrated-confidence multi-candidate emission"  # 8620
    status: violated  # 8620
    priority: 3  # 8620
    parent:  # 8620
    tags:  # 8620
    - analysis_quality  # 8620
    - cross_language_linkers  # 8620
    - kind_segregated_registry  # 8620
    isbefore: []  # 8620
    duplicate_of: []  # 8620
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 8620
    pr_ref:  # 8620
    description: "## Statement (also in `fields.statement`)\n\nWhen an analyzer or linker name-resolution pass encounters multiple symbol candidates for a single name reference (call site, instantiation, type use, dispatch), it must either:\n\n- **(a)** pick the correct candidate via available `kind` / scope / type / `path_hint` / namespace context, OR\n- **(b)** return all candidates with calibrated confidence so downstream filtering, ranking, or surfacing can express the ambiguity honestly.\n\nSingle-value, name-keyed dictionaries that silently overwrite-on-insert or pick-last-inserted are a **violated form** of this invariant — they collapse N candidates into 1 with no signal about the loss.\n\nTwo solution shapes are both valid:\n\n- **Kind-segregated multi-value registry** — store all candidates keyed by `(name, kind)` or `name → list[symbol]`; lookup applies kind preference / context-based selection. Reference: Rust BUG-04 fix (`rust.py` post-`WI-milak`), VHDL post-`WI-morud`. Conceptual ancestor in the codebase: `inheritance.py:51-67`.\n- **Calibrated-confidence multi-candidate emission** — emit all candidates with confidence scaled by ambiguity (`1/sqrt(N)`-style) so high-ambiguity false positives drop below downstream filter thresholds. Reference: `INV-rovuj` (C++ method lookup on receivers, via `symbol_resolution.py:227,272,595,872` + `cpp.py:837` `path_hint` threading).\n\n## Why this is an invariant, not a per-analyzer task\n\n`WI-morud`'s description articulates the escalation rule that motivated this META: *\"If a third analogous bug surfaces in a third analyzer, escalate to a refactor that introduces a shared kind-segregated multi-value dict utility (cf. `inheritance.py:51-67`). Until then, per-analyzer targeted fixes are cheaper.\"*\n\nThat rule has fired. `INV-paroh` is the third sibling in the BUG-04 lineage (Rust → VHDL → Verilog), and there's a broader cluster of done items at `WI-jifiv` / `WI-rusuh` / `WI-patiz` / `WI-fujum` / `WI-sifiz` showing the same pattern across HTTP / gRPC / DI / FFI linkers. The trigger condition WI-morud anticipated is no longer hypothetical.\n\n## Members initially parented under this META\n\n- `WI-milak` (P1 done) — Rust BUG-04 originating instance. `rust.py:1097` single-value `global_symbols` dict misresolved trait/struct name collisions. Closed via multi-value kind-segregated registry with trait-preference rule.\n- `WI-morud` (P3 done) — VHDL `architecture-of-entity` lookup. Sibling to BUG-04; same fix shape per-analyzer.\n- `INV-paroh` (P4 violated) — Verilog `module-instantiation` lookup. **Third sibling, escalation trigger.** Author's framing offers two paths: (A) targeted per-analyzer fix mirroring WI-morud, or (B) refactor escalation to a shared `KindSegregatedRegistry` utility. Decision should be made WITH this META filed, not before it.\n- `INV-rovuj` (P3 satisfied) — C++ method lookup on receivers. **Satisfied flagship for the calibrated-confidence solution branch.** Demonstrates the alternate `1/sqrt(N)` + `path_hint` shape; reference for cases where compile-quality type info is unavailable and the kind-segregated approach can't fully disambiguate.\n\n## Closely related instances (not initially parented; see \"Open question\")\n\nThe same lookup-disambiguation shape appears in five additional already-closed items, surfaced during the BUG-04/05 audit campaign:\n\n- `WI-jifiv` (P2 done) — subprocess_cli linker `command_by_name`\n- `WI-rusuh` (P2 done) — route_handler linker `symbol_by_name` primary index\n- `WI-patiz` (P2 done) — grpc-linker `service_sym_by_name`\n- `WI-fujum` (P2 done) — di_resolution NestJS `sym_by_name`\n- `WI-sifiz` (P3 done) — PyFFI linker dual-lookup conformance (also references `INV-zuhub` item 1)\n\nThese all solved the same pattern with variations of the kind-segregated approach. Whether they're parented under this META is a curatorial choice — see \"Open question.\"\n\n## Related but separate axis\n\n`INV-zuhub` (P2 pending_validation) — *\"Linker simple-name fallback edges must carry confidence ≤ 0.5 + disambiguation_fallback flag.\"* This is the **edge-side contract** when a linker falls back to short-name match — i.e., it specifies how to flag the result when the lookup mechanism this META covers cannot disambiguate cleanly. Producer/consumer pairing:\n\n- *This META*: defines how the lookup mechanism should behave (don't silently overwrite; either pick correctly or return all candidates).\n- `INV-zuhub`: defines how the resulting edges should be marked (low confidence + provenance flag) when the lookup returned multi-candidate.\n\nBoth are required for a coherent disambiguation contract; neither subsumes the other.\n\n## Closure criterion\n\nEach analyzer or linker name-resolution pass that does name-keyed lookup must satisfy one of:\n\n- (a) Pick correctly via kind/scope/type/path_hint context, OR\n- (b) Return all candidates with calibrated confidence (the `INV-rovuj` shape).\n\nSingle-value-dict-with-silent-overwrite is a violated form. Existing instances close as their per-language fix lands (kind-segregated OR confidence-calibrated). New analyzers must pick a shape and document it; the META closes `satisfied` once every active name-resolution pass has been audited and assigned a shape.\n\nThe shared `KindSegregatedRegistry` utility implied by `WI-morud`'s escalation rule — and demanded by the BUG-04/05 audit-cohort breadth — is the natural follow-on work to attach as a child WI of this META if pursued.\n\n## Open question for the agent or maintainer that picks this up\n\n**Should `WI-jifiv` / `WI-rusuh` / `WI-patiz` / `WI-fujum` / `WI-sifiz` also be parented under this META?** Arguments either way:\n\n- *Yes*: they are exact instances of the same shape; making them children gives the META a complete historical catalogue of the family and makes future audit findings (the BUG-04/05 shape recurrence pattern) findable from one entry point.\n- *No*: they're already closed, and re-parenting closed items is a churn-without-action move. Description-level reference (as above) may be sufficient.\n\nRecorded here so the next reader has the context to decide."  # 8620
    fields:  # 8620
      statement: "When an analyzer or linker name-resolution pass encounters multiple symbol candidates for a single name reference, it must either (a) pick the correct candidate via available kind/scope/type/path_hint/namespace context, OR (b) return all candidates with calibrated confidence so downstream filtering or surfacing can express the ambiguity honestly. Single-value name-keyed dictionaries that silently overwrite-on-insert or pick-last-inserted are a violated form."  # 8620
      root_cause: "Per-analyzer and per-linker name-resolution passes historically implement their own lookup mechanism. The naive shape — Python dict keyed by name with single-value insertion — silently picks last-inserted when names collide, producing wrong edges with no signal that disambiguation was needed. The BUG-04 lineage (Rust/VHDL/Verilog) and the BUG-04/05 audit cohort (subprocess_cli/route_handler/grpc/di_resolution/PyFFI) are recurrences of the same shape across different code areas. WI-morud articulated the escalation rule (three siblings → shared utility) before this META existed; that trigger condition is now met."  # 8620
